---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import DownloadButton from '../components/DownloadButton.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<Base title="Download Kikimi" description="Download Kikimi for Mac, how to open it the first time, and what it needs." current="download">
  <h1 style="margin-top: 40px">Download Kikimi</h1>
  <p class="lead">Free, open source and a beta. It runs on your Mac and needs no account.</p>
  <DownloadButton />
  <p class="small">The button opens the Releases page on GitHub, where each version has its zip file and a checksum to check it against.</p>

  <h2>What you need</h2>
  <ul class="list">
    <li>A Mac with macOS 15 or later. Apple silicon is recommended; speech models run much faster on it.</li>
    <li>Translations need macOS 26. On macOS 15 to 25 every translation control is hidden and nothing else changes.</li>
    <li>About 1.5 GB of free space for a speech model (1.6 GB for Japanese), only if you want Kikimi to transcribe videos that have no subtitles. A video with an <code>.srt</code> or <code>.vtt</code> file needs no model.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Opening it the first time</h2>
  <Callout title="macOS will say it can't verify the app">
    <p>That is expected. Kikimi is free and open source, and it is not notarized yet (Apple charges a yearly fee for that).</p>
  </Callout>
  <ol class="list">
    <li>Unzip the download and drag <strong>Kikimi</strong> into Applications.</li>
    <li>Open it. When macOS shows the warning, click <strong>Done</strong>.</li>
    <li>Open <strong>System Settings &gt; Privacy &amp; Security</strong>, scroll to the message about Kikimi, click <strong>Open Anyway</strong> and confirm.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    If you prefer Terminal, this removes the download flag once. Do it only for a copy you trust, since it skips macOS's check:
  </p>
  <pre><code>xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine /Applications/Kikimi.app</code></pre>
  <p>Building from source avoids the warning altogether; the <a href="https://github.com/surreptus/kikimi#build-from-source">README</a> explains how.</p>

  <h2>Check the download</h2>
  <p>Each release lists a SHA-256 checksum. In Terminal, run this on the file you downloaded and compare it with the one in the notes:</p>
  <pre><code>shasum -a 256 ~/Downloads/Kikimi-*-macOS.zip</code></pre>
  <p>A matching checksum shows you have the file that was published, not that it is safe. The code is public, so you can read it or build the same version yourself.</p>

  <h2>Updates</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Kikimi &gt; Check for Updates</strong> reads one small file from this website, only when you choose it, and sends nothing
    else. There is no automatic updating. See the <a href={`${base}/changelog/`}>changelog</a> for what changed.
  </p>

  <h2>Next</h2>
  <p>Start with <a href={`${base}/guide/getting-started/`}>the first-session guide</a>.</p>
</Base>
